[Music]  mitsubishi has given the Lancer a  makeover among the features are a  front-end facelift new rims and an  updated interior it's available in sedan  or Sportback models we tested the sport  back the new front has a wide front  bumper painted in the same color of the  car and a horizontal radiator grille  with chrome  [Music]  what's more there are new headlights  with LED daytime running lights on the  sides this feature however is standard  only in the plus and top equipment  packages  a look at the Lancer Sportback from the  side reveals a flaw that's remained in  the revamped model some team car test a  Manuel shaver says the car still has  blind spots  just like before its facelift the Lancer  had very wide c-pillars a small rear  window and the back seat headrests are  difficult to lower this makes it  difficult for the driver to see what's  behind the car thank goodness a reverse  assist camera is available as an option  yet if you want this driving aid you've  got to choose the plus package or higher  Mitsubishi amanna wa says Mitsubishi has  taken some of the criticism of its  customers too hard because whereas the  previous model at only 270 cubic leaders  of trunk space a new Lancer can fit far  more cargo with 400 in the interior  still has clean line says Amano L with  only a few climate control knobs the  touchscreen does the rest but it's a  shame even the more expensive models  still don't come with a sat-nav golf  place via Honda for the manual says the  car is comfortable but he finds that the  sides of the seats are far too soft all  the advice that's when the car is  cornering you're sort of thrown off  balance the advice is making the sides  of the seats firmer or redesigning the  seating it's an infestation of skeet  the engines are known quantities they  both run on gasoline and produce 86 and  103 kilowatts of power respectively  we're testing the smaller 1.6 liter  model it can take the Lancer Sportback  from zero to one hundred and ten point  seven seconds with a maximum speed of  191 km/h the manufacturer says it uses  5.5 liters of fuel to travel 100  kilometers  [Music]  man whoa says the Lancer Sportback comes  with either a 1.8 liter gasoline engine  with a CVT automatic or what we're  driving a 1.6 liter 5-speed manual  he says the 5-speed is outdated most  cars in this range already have  six-speed transmissions it remains to be  seen whether the facelifted Lancer will  be carried on for another generation  because Mitsubishi is already planning a  midsize sedan for its renault-nissan  Alliance industry analysts say this  model could follow the Lancer perhaps  under a new name as well

